35:["$","$L45",null,{"children":[["$","$L46",null,{"title":"Can I split the config across multiple files?","id":"can-i-split-the-config-across-multiple-files","children":["$","p",null,{"children":["Not with an include directive; the parser reads exactly one file. What you do\ninstead is split by ",["$","em",null,{"children":"role"}],": one config for the CAS process, one for the\nscheduler, one for the workers, each a complete file naming the others by\nnetwork address. That's the same split described in\n",["$","$L6",null,{"href":"/configuration","children":"the section overview"}],", and it's how the docker-compose\ndeployment is laid out."]}]}],["$","$L46",null,{"title":"How do I keep secrets out of the file?","id":"how-do-i-keep-secrets-out-of-the-file","children":["$","p",null,{"children":["Use environment substitution for the fields that carry them and set the\nvariables from your platform's secret mechanism: a Kubernetes Secret mounted\nas environment variables, a systemd ",["$","code",null,{"children":"EnvironmentFile"}],", or your process\nsupervisor's equivalent. The config file itself then contains a variable name,\nwhich is safe to commit."]}]}],["$","$L46",null,{"title":"Why did my config work as JSON but not as JSON5, or vice versa?","id":"why-did-my-config-work-as-json-but-not-as-json5-or-vice-versa","children":["$","p",null,{"children":"Every valid JSON document is also a valid JSON5 document, so that direction\nnever breaks. The other direction does: a config using comments or trailing\ncommas will be rejected by any strict-JSON tooling you point at it. If a\nlinter, formatter, or CI check complains about a file that Forge accepts\nhappily, the tool is the thing that's misconfigured."}]}],["$","$L46",null,{"title":"The process starts, but nothing works. Was the config valid?","id":"the-process-starts-but-nothing-works-was-the-config-valid","children":["$","p",null,{"children":["Yes, and that's the boundary worth knowing. Parsing checks structure and\nspelling. It does not check that a store name referenced by a service exists\nin a useful state, that a worker can reach the scheduler address you gave it,\nor that a path is writable. Those are runtime failures, and they show up in\nlogs rather than at startup. ",["$","$L6",null,{"href":"/operate/observability","children":"Observability"}]," is where\nyou go to read them."]}]}]]}]
